---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const path = '/mm/';

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How small is a millimeter?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A millimeter is one thousandth of a meter — roughly the thickness of a credit card (0.76 mm) or a little less than half the thickness of a US dime (1.35 mm). It is the smallest unit most people measure by eye.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Should I use mm or cm mode?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'They show the same scale with different labels. Use mm mode when you want a single precise number like 47 mm; use cm mode when you think in centimeters like 4.7 cm. For anything under about 5 cm, mm mode is usually easier to read.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How accurate is the on-screen millimeter scale?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'As accurate as your calibration. With the credit-card method, expect accuracy within about half a millimeter on a typical display. For sub-millimeter work — jewelry, electronics — use proper calipers; a screen ruler is a quick check, not a metrology tool.',
      },
    },
  ],
};

const related = [
  { href: '/cm/', label: 'Centimeter ruler' },
  { href: '/inches/', label: 'Inch ruler' },
  { href: '/guide/', label: 'How to read the ruler' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Online Millimeter Ruler — Measure in mm at True Size | Real Online Ruler"
  description="A free on-screen millimeter ruler at true physical size. Calibrate once, then read precise millimeter measurements with labeled 10 mm marks."
  path={path}
  h1="Millimeter ruler"
  lede="When centimeters are too coarse, millimeters take over — one tenth of a centimeter, small enough for screws, gaps, and ring sizes. Calibrate once, switch to mm, and every tiny tick on your screen is a true millimeter."
  breadcrumb="Millimeter ruler"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
  related={related}
>
  <h2>What is a millimeter?</h2>
  <p>
    A millimeter is one thousandth of a meter and one tenth of a centimeter — the smallest unit most
    people comfortably measure by eye. Anything thinner than a millimeter (paper is about 0.1 mm)
    belongs to calipers and micrometers; everything from about 1 mm up to a few centimeters is
    millimeter territory.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the mm scale</h2>
  <p>
    In mm mode, <strong>every small tick is one millimeter</strong> and the long numbered ticks come
    every 10 mm, labeled 10, 20, 30… Read the numbered mark below the object's end, then count the
    small ticks past it: four ticks past 40 is <strong>44 mm</strong>. The medium-length tick at
    each 5 is the half centimeter — a handy landmark when counting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scale is identical to <a href="/cm/">cm mode</a>; only the labels differ. Switch freely — 44
    mm and 4.4 cm are the same mark.
  </p>

  <h2>Handy size references</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Credit / bank card thickness — <strong>0.76 mm</strong> (an exact standard)
    </li>
    <li>
      US dime thickness — about <strong>1.35 mm</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      SD card thickness — about <strong>2.1 mm</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      Standard pencil diameter — about <strong>7 mm</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      US quarter diameter — <strong>24.26 mm</strong>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conversions</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>From</th>
        <th>To</th>
        <th>Multiply by</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>mm</td>
        <td>cm</td>
        <td>0.1</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>mm</td>
        <td>m</td>
        <td>0.001</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>mm</td>
        <td>inches</td>
        <td>0.03937</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>inches</td>
        <td>mm</td>
        <td>25.4 (exact)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Getting precise results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Calibrate with the card method.</strong> Millimeter accuracy lives or dies on
      calibration — the physical-card method is the most accurate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Zoom the page to 100% and leave it there.</strong> Any zoom rescales the ticks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use guides for small objects.</strong> Drop a guide at each end of the object and
      subtract the readouts — steadier than eyeballing a tick.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>View straight on.</strong> Parallax at an angle can shift an apparent edge by a
      millimeter or more.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How small is a millimeter?</h3>
  <p>
    A millimeter is one thousandth of a meter — roughly the thickness of a credit card (0.76 mm) or
    a little less than half the thickness of a US dime (1.35 mm). It is the smallest unit most
    people measure by eye.
  </p>
  <h3>Should I use mm or cm mode?</h3>
  <p>
    They show the same scale with different labels. Use mm mode when you want a single precise
    number like 47 mm; use cm mode when you think in centimeters like 4.7 cm. For anything under
    about 5 cm, mm mode is usually easier to read.
  </p>
  <h3>How accurate is the on-screen millimeter scale?</h3>
  <p>
    As accurate as your calibration. With the credit-card method, expect accuracy within about half
    a millimeter on a typical display. For sub-millimeter work — jewelry, electronics — use proper
    calipers; a screen ruler is a quick check, not a metrology tool.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
